WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.680
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.740
This video’s going to be purely instructional
because your YouTube experience could be better

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:05.540
if you knew some YouTube secrets.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.350
For example, I just flashed some text on the
screen, but you couldn’t read it because

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.970
it was only there for one frame, but what
are you gonna do?

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.130
You gonna go back and then try to pause at
the exact right second?

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.010
No! You're not!

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:16.940
Instead, you’re gonna watch this video,
which has 18 tips for how to use YouTube better,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.410
including how to find that text.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.120
First, though, let’s talk about subscriptions.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.800
If you’re logged in to YouTube and you go
to youtube.com, you will find a page of content

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.080
that YouTube has decided that you probably
will want to watch.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.160
It will probably keep you on the site.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.600
But it will not be a feed of videos that you
control.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.340
The only way to get to that is to click on
this little weird icon at the top and go to

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.400
subscriptions.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.860
Also under that icon is your history, so you
can see videos you recently watched, and your

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:43.550
watch later playlist, which is a wonderful
thing because often you see a video that you

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.650
want to watch but you don’t have time to
watch at that exact moment.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.130
Every YouTube video has a little clock.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.350
If you hover over it, you can click that,
it adds to your watch later playlist.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.160
Then you can click on that either on your
desktop or on your phone, whatever you want,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.190
and watch those videos.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.240
The majority of nerdfighters who answered
the nerdfighteria census get their YouTube

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:04.000
videos from the subscriptions feed, and that
is wonderful for us because it’s the only

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.780
page on YouTube where you can go and actually
make sure you’re seeing all the content

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:08.780
your favorite creators are making.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.610
But the subscription page, if you got a lot
of subscriptions, like I do, can be a bit

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
of a fire hose.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.580
It makes it easier to miss content from the
people I never want to miss stuff from, which

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.560
is why, for certain channels, I have notifications
turned on.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.909
It’s a little bell thing, you click on it,
and decide how you want to get the notification.

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:26.250
You can also do this from your subscription
manager page.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.020
You can see all the people you’re subscribed
to, maybe take out the ones that you’re

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.020
not watching so much anymore.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.770
And for the ones you never want to miss a
video from, you can turn on notifications.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.940
You can also set up notifications on your
phone—the little bell on a channel page.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.920
You can get push notifications every time
a new video gets uploaded, and you can join

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.150
that coveted group, the notification squad,
if you’ve ever seen that comment.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.310
Your subscription feed is actually easier
to find on mobile, it’s one of the big buttons

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:51.110
at the top, and now that some YouTubers, including
us, have the ability to post community posts

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.870
that aren’t videos but will show up in your
mobile subscription feed, you can decide whether

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.920
or not you want to see those community posts.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.020
For now, I’d suggest turning that on, trying
it out, because we’re gonna be messing

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.640
around with it some in the coming weeks.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.840
This is all fine and good when you’re on
your phone or on your computer, but what about

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.189
if you wanna be on the big screen?

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:10.810
Well, if you have any kind of smart-enabled
television that’s sharing a Wi-Fi connection

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.911
with your phone or your computer, a little
icon will show up, and you can click it, and

00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:17.360
send your YouTube straight to your TV.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.190
And if you’re on your phone while you’re
watching the video, you can queue up the next

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.060
video and the next video and then, it’s
great!

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.400
It’s frickin—it works like a dream!

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.570
Youtube has its problems; this is not one
of them.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:27.980
I use it all the time.

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:30.989
Now, finally, back to desktop to talk about
hot keys.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:33.969
Now, because of a weird confluence of things
that’s not really worth talking about, sometimes

00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:36.449
if you press the space bar, it will pause
the video.

00:02:36.449 --> 00:02:40.280
Sometimes it will jump down to the comment
box, which will be extremely frustrating,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.010
‘cause then you have to scroll up and then
press pause, and you’re like, why didn’t

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.310
the space bar just work?

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:50.739
But if you push the “K” key, it will always
pause the video.

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:54.220
“L” will advance 10 seconds, and “J”
will go back 10 seconds.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.200
These keys being used for this purpose is
actually a holdover from old video editing software,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.891
and a lot of people think that it’s
annoying and weird and like it doesn’t make

00:03:01.891 --> 00:03:04.700
any sense that it’s those particular keys,
but I actually kind of love it.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.310
And there are a bunch of other hot keys.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:10.140
“M” will mute the video, the up and down
arrows will make the volume go up and down,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.400
the left and right arrows will advance and
reverse the video 5 seconds.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.440
The numbers on your keyboard will advance
the video to that point in the video, so if

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.629
you like hit “6,” it will go to 60% of
the way through the video.

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:20.630
If you hit “0,” it will go back to the
beginning.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.172
The “greater than” or “less than”
keys will speed up or slow down the video.

00:03:23.172 --> 00:03:28.952
And finally, the period and comma will advance
and reverse one frame at a time,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.280
allowing you finally to catch that weird one frame
of text that the creator decided

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.980
to put on the screen just to mess with you.

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.231
If you’re on your TV or phone, though I
got—I got nothing for you.

00:03:38.231 --> 00:03:40.451
You just gotta pause and unpause real quick.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.780
If anybody else has any tips or tricks for
how they use YouTube most efficiently,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.620
I really wanna see those in the comments.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.520
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

